###LOVE#SCAB###
##PORES#ARGON##
#BABBIT#MEEKER#
BIG#SLAPPFD#COO
AGOG#STALL#STUD
SODAS#ERE#STAGE
STARTS#K#SPIRES
###TOIL#SOAP###
USHERS#H#TRENCH
SPARK#FAD#SNORE
ERRS#DOMES#DOER
DUE#CORSETS#SID
#EMBALM#DIADEM#
##SANTA#ELVES##
###TEST#DEEM###


A1. A disease that is common in the spring and is usually accompanied by temporary insanity. ~ LOVE
A5. A target for union bricklayers to throw bricks at. ~ SCAB
A9. Small openings in the skin. ~ PORES
A10. A gaseous constituent of the atmosphere. ~ ARGON
A12. A main street resident who joins the Rotarians, goes to the movies Saturday night, and hides razor blades on top of the medicine cabinet. ~ BABBIT
A13. More submissive. ~ MEEKER
A15. Large. ~ BIG
A16. What a fellow gets when he disregards a stop signal. ~ SLAPPFD
A18. Something turtle doves do. ~ COO
A20. The way a girl gets when someone tells her they have a trade-last for her. ~ AGOG
A22. What do autos do at railroad crossings? ~ STALL
A23. A stiff kind of poker. ~ STUD
A24. Favorite drinks of drug-store cowboys. ~ SODAS
A26. Here in England (phonetic). ~ ERE
A27. Something that bad actors often go on. ~ STAGE
A28. What a sprinter does before he can finish a race. ~ STARTS
A30. Poor places for aviators to land. ~ SPIRES
A31. Hard work. ~ TOIL
A32. The only sure thing for a clean-up. ~ SOAP
A33. A bridegroom's friendly enemies. ~ USHERS
A37. Before the Armistice was signed this was a dangerous thing to get into and even more dangerous to get out of. ~ TRENCH
A41. To neck (almost obsolete). ~ SPARK
A42. Something Mah Jongg was, and crosswords are. ~ FAD
A44. Sheet music. ~ SNORE
A45. Mistakes. ~ ERRS
A46. Things often made of solid ivory. ~ DOMES
A48. One who does. ~ DOER
A49. What your income tax is on March 15. ~ DUE
A50. Shapers (also almost obsolete). ~ CORSETS
A52. Unhappy. ~ SID
A53. To preserve for a long, long rest. ~ EMBALM
A55. A crown (not the kind you get from friend wife). ~ DIADEM
A57. The first name of Mr. Claus. ~ SANTA
A58. Mischievous sprites. ~ ELVES
A59. To try by experiment (as taking a drink of cabaret hooch). ~ TEST
A60. What a Congressman says when he means think. ~ DEEM

D1. A high ball (this is hard to get!). ~ LOB
D2. This is what poets oft call a maiden's eyes. ~ ORBS
D3. Nets that have helped catch a lot of poor fish. ~ VEILS
D4. Something there's not much left of after the lawyers get through disputing a will. ~ ESTATE
D5. Something a bootlegger gives you that's a lot different than what he sells you. ~ SAMPLE
D6. Something fishermen carry with them that they seldom need. ~ CREFL
D7. What you would become if you worked crossword puzzles for ninety-seven years. ~ AGED
D8. A man who was willing to pay for peace. ~ BOK
D9. A sacred tower in the Orient. ~ PAGODA
D11. The drink of the gods. (This was before Mr. Volstead's time.) ~ NECTAR
D12. Anyone who disagrees with you about religion. ~ BIGOT
D14. You tell 'em, my lips stick! ~ ROUGE
D15. A low tone. ~ BASS
D17. Something a motorist can't do where he wants to. ~ PARK
D19. Poets turn these out by the ream. ~ ODES
D21. These are hold-ups. ~ GARTERS
D23. A substitute for salary. ~ STIPEND
D25. There's no closed season on this bird. ~ STORK
D27. Things to cling to if you're in a wreck. ~ SPARS
D29. Pet name for sister. ~ SIS
D30. A drunkard. ~ SOT
D33. The kind of cigars a snipe-shooter smokes. ~ USED
D34. A projection from a casting that is designed to be removed after the cast is made. ~ SPRUE
D35. Turkish delights. ~ HAREMS
D36. Bad actors. (If we only had some eggs now.) ~ HAMS
D38. Neckties of unpopular design. ~ NOOSES
D39. Trade name for restaurant mixture of milk and water. ~ CREIM
D40. A bunch of cattle (did you ever ride in a subway?). ~ HERD
D42. The form, size and general get-up of a book. ~ FORMAT
D43. Conveyed by deed. ~ DEEDED
D46. Polite term for city officials. ~ DOLTS
D47. One way to get over a fence with dignity. ~ STILE
D50. Makes walking easy. ~ CANE
D51. Sure method of getting rich according to the bankers. ~ SAVE
D54. An animal that sleeps upside down. ~ BAT
D56. A member of a formerly great political party (abr.). ~ DEM


